course: everyday
lesson: 115
title: Fire, candles and garage and garden tools
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Fire grows very fast: a candle flame that touches a curtain can fill a room with smoke in a few minutes, which is why matches, lighters and candles are grown-ups' tools and are never left alone.
explain: In a fire, things do not matter and people do: you get out at once, leave everything behind and tell the firefighters about any pet still inside.
explain: The garage and garden hold a second set of dangers: blades that cut in an instant, poisons that look harmless, ladders that fall, water that drowns, and car exhaust with invisible carbon monoxide.
explain: For example, antifreeze tastes sweet, so a child or a dog might drink it, but even a little can kill; this is why garage chemicals are never touched.
explain: Many garden accidents are simple to prevent: lay a rake with its teeth down, wear gloves, stay well away from the mower and empty the paddling pool after use.
fact: Matches and lighters are tools for grown-ups and should be given to a grown-up if found.
fact: A lit candle must never be left alone, must be kept away from curtains, and is blown out before you leave the room.
fact: Hair catches fire easily, so keep it back and blow birthday candles from a safe distance.
fact: In a fire you leave everything and get out, and you tell firefighters about pets instead of going back.
fact: In a fire, get low under the smoke, get out, stay out and call the emergency number.
fact: Children stay behind the fire guard and do not put things into a fire without a grown-up.
fact: Fireworks are watched from a safe distance, a used firework is never picked up, and sparklers are held with gloves and put in water after.
fact: Lawnmowers throw stones and their blades can cut off toes, so children stay well away.
fact: A rake left with its teeth up can swing its handle into your face when stepped on.
fact: Power tools such as drills, saws and hedge trimmers cut and crush in an instant.
fact: Weedkiller, slug pellets, paint, petrol and antifreeze are poisons, and antifreeze tastes sweet but is deadly.
fact: Car exhaust contains carbon monoxide, a poisonous gas you cannot see or smell.
fact: Children climb a ladder only when a grown-up says yes and holds it, because ladders can slip and fall.
fact: Gardening gloves protect your hands from thorns, splinters, germs in the soil and insect stings.
fact: Young children can drown in very shallow water, so paddling pools are emptied after use.
exercise: You find a lighter on the coffee table. What should you do? | Give it to a grown-up without playing with it | Click it a few times to check whether it still works properly | Hide it in your bedroom | Try it out in the garden
exercise: You light a candle in your bedroom with your mum. What must you remember? | Never leave it alone and blow it out before leaving the room | Put it on the windowsill near the curtains so it looks pretty outside | Leave it burning so the room stays bright | Put it by your bed to read by
exercise: Why should you tie long hair back before blowing out birthday candles? | Hair catches fire very easily | Hair makes the candles go out too soon | Hair falls into the cake | It helps you blow harder
exercise: The smoke alarm goes off and there is smoke in the hall. Your tablet is upstairs. What do you do? | Leave it and get out at once | Run up quickly to fetch it | Wait by the door for the smoke to clear | Hide under your bed with it
exercise: Your cat is still inside the burning house. What is the right thing to do? | Tell the firefighters, but do not go back in | Run back in to find her | Call her name loudly from just inside the hallway door | Go back in with a wet towel
exercise: There is an open fire in the living room. What is safe? | Stay behind the fire guard and do not throw things in | Poke the fire with a long stick to make the flames grow bigger | Sit right up close to keep warm | Throw paper in to see it burn
exercise: How should you hold a sparkler on Bonfire Night or the Fourth of July? | With gloves, at arm's length, then put it in water after | With bare hands close to your face | By the hot end so it stays still | Two at a time, one in each hand, waving them at your friends
exercise: A firework did not go off. What should you do? | Never go back to it or pick it up | Pick it up to see what went wrong | Light it again from close by | Put it in your pocket for later
exercise: Your dad is mowing the lawn. Where should you be? | Well away from the mower | Right behind it to help push | In front of it picking up toys | Sitting on the grass nearby
exercise: You see a rake lying on the lawn with its teeth pointing up. What should you do? | Turn it teeth down or hang it up | Walk over it carefully | Leave it for someone else | Push the teeth into the ground handle up
exercise: Your uncle leaves a hedge trimmer on the garage floor. Can you try it? | No, power tools cut and crush in an instant | Yes, if you hold it with both hands | Yes, as long as it has not been plugged in for very long at all | Yes, if you only cut a small branch
exercise: You have been helping with slug pellets in the garden. What should you do after? | Wash your hands well | Wipe your hands on the dog | Eat a snack straight away | Nothing, slug pellets are only for slugs
exercise: A bright green liquid in the garage smells sweet. Why must you never taste it? | It may be antifreeze, which tastes sweet but is deadly | Sweet things are always good for you | It is only juice that has gone off | It will only turn your teeth and tongue green for a day or two
exercise: Why should you never play in a garage while a car engine is running? | The exhaust has carbon monoxide, a poisonous gas | The car might start playing music | The engine makes the air in the garage much too cold to play in | The noise hurts the car
exercise: A ladder is leaning against the house. When can you climb it? | Only with a grown-up holding it and saying yes | Whenever nobody is watching | Only if you climb very fast | Only if you hold on with one hand
exercise: Why wear gloves when gardening? | They protect from thorns, splinters, soil germs and insects | They make the plants grow faster | They keep the soil warm | They stop the gardener's hands from sweating on hot summer days
exercise: The paddling pool still has water in it after the party. What should happen? | Empty it, because small children can drown in shallow water | Leave it full until tomorrow so that no water gets wasted at all | Cover it with a towel | Let the toddler play in it alone
test: A girl in Dublin falls asleep with a candle burning on her shelf. What could happen? | The candle could set things alight and start a house fire | The candle goes out by itself as soon as she sleeps | The room just gets too warm | Nothing, candles are only dangerous outdoors
test: Why do firefighters tell people never to go back into a burning house? | People die going back inside for things or pets | Firefighters want to save the things themselves | Houses get locked when there is a fire | Smoke is only dangerous outside
test: Which garage chemical is especially dangerous to pets and children because it tastes sweet? | Antifreeze | Water | Builder's sand | Sawdust
test: A boy in Melbourne wants to collect the stones in front of the mower while his mum mows. Why is this wrong? | The mower can throw stones and its blades can cut feet | The mower will stop working | It makes the grass grow back much faster and thicker afterwards | It scares the birds away
test: Why is carbon monoxide from a car so dangerous? | You cannot see or smell it, but it poisons you | It is always thick black smoke | It only hurts the car | It smells so strongly of rotten eggs that everyone runs away
test: Why is a garden pond a danger for a toddler even if it is shallow? | Young children can drown in very shallow water | Ponds are only dangerous in winter when the ice is thin | Shallow water cannot hurt anyone | The fish might bite the toddler
test: What should happen to a used sparkler? | It goes into a bucket of water | It goes straight into the rubbish bin | It is given to a younger child | It is left on the grass
test: Ben in Colorado steps on a rake and the handle hits his nose. How could it have been avoided? | By laying the rake teeth down | By using a longer rake | By raking faster | By wearing gardening gloves
test: Which is true about matches? | They are tools for grown-ups, and a fire can start very fast | They are safe little toys for children if you are very careful | Only wooden matches can start fires | Matches cannot start fires indoors
test: found a lighter on the bus seat, keep it? | Give it to a grown-up | Keep it for camping | Try it once to see if it works | Put it in your pocket for later
test: candle in my room is on, going downstairs 4 a bit | Blow it out first | Leave it, it'll be fine | Move it near the curtain so it's out of the way | Put a book on top of it
test: the cats still inside and the house is on fire!! | Get out and tell the firefighters about the cat | Run back in to grab her | Call her name from inside the hall, then run back out | Go back in with a wet towel
test: firework didn't go off, can i go look | No, never go back to a used firework | Yes, after one minute | Yes, pick it up and relight it | Kick it to check it
explain: A girl in Ohio fell asleep with a candle burning on her shelf; it lit the curtains, and the family escaped only because the smoke alarm went off.
explain: A boy in Australia left a rake lying teeth-up on the lawn; he stepped on it in the evening, the handle flew up and hit his face, and he needed stitches above his eye.
fact: Smoke alarms give families time to escape, and most home fire deaths happen in homes without a working alarm.
fact: Candles left burning near curtains or while people sleep are a common cause of house fires.
test: candle on my shelf, leaving it on while i sleep | Blow it out, it can set things on fire | Leave it, candles go out alone | Move it next to the curtain | Only leave it if it's small
test: smoke alarm beeping cos battery low, can i just take it out | No, tell an adult to change the battery, alarms save lives | Yes, take it out, the beeping is annoying and it's fine without | Yes, it's not needed at night | Only put it back in winter
test: fire in the house and smoke everywhere | Get low, get out, stay out, call the emergency number | Hide under the bed | Go back for your toys | Open every door in the house to look for where the fire is
test: did u hear the Smiths' house burnt down cos of a candle n "the flames were 50 metres high". what's probs exaggerated | The 50 metres; candles near curtains really do start fires | That a candle can set curtains alight | That a smoke alarm can give people enough time to get out safely | That fire spreads through a room fast
test: kid in my class says his uncle went back in a fire for the cat n he's a hero, so u should too. real lesson? | No, get out and tell firefighters about the pet | Yes, go back quickly for any pets | Yes, if the fire is only downstairs | Yes, hold your breath and run in
test: my brother says a boy's dog drank antifreeze in the garage n it's the boy's fault, everyone's saying it. pass it on? | No, it is unkind; just know antifreeze is sweet but deadly | Yes, so everyone knows that he is the one who killed his dog | Yes, but only to people with dogs | Yes, because it is a true story
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antifreeze presents a particular danger to children and dogs. | It tastes sweet, yet even a little can kill | It is bright blue and therefore easily seen | It is safe in small sips but not in large ones | It has no harm unless it is heated first
exam: Whereas a fire has broken out in a dwelling, state the course of conduct to be followed in respect of pets remaining inside. | Leave and tell the firefighters about the pets | Go back inside to fetch the pets quickly | Stay inside the house until all of the pets have been found | Open every window and call the pets loudly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises carbon monoxide contained in car exhaust? | It is poisonous and can be neither seen nor smelt | It is a harmless gas that merely smells of petrol | It is dangerous only when it is visible as smoke | It is poisonous only when the car is moving fast
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the use of paddling pools by young children. | They are emptied after use, as toddlers drown in shallow water | They are left full overnight to keep the water warm | They are safe without supervision as the water is shallow | They are covered with a blanket and left full for next time
